{"id":"neet-2022r-q066","pn":66,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Surface Chemistry","st":"Adsorption isotherms","q":"Shown below are adsorption isotherms for a gas 'X' at temperatures T₁, T₂ and T₃ :\np and x/m represent pressure and extent of adsorption, respectively. The correct order of temperatures for the given, adsorption is :","o":["T₁ = T₂ > T₃","T₁ > T₂ > T₃","T₃ > T₂ > T₁","T₁ = T₂ = T₃"],"c":2,"s":"As temperature rises, adsorption of a gas on a solid falls.\nThe curve for T₁ is highest and T₃ is lowest, so T₁ has the most adsorption.\nHence T₃ > T₂ > T₁.","fig":{"w":260,"h":170,"alt":"Adsorption isotherms: x/m against P for T1, T2 and T3, three curves rising from the origin and levelling off, T1 highest and T3 lowest.","d":[["l",50,140,50,14,"a"],["l",50,140,232,140,"a"],["t",44,70,"x/m","e"],["t",140,162,"P","m"],["p","M50 140C70 78 108 44 188 32",""],["p","M50 140C72 92 108 64 188 55",""],["p","M50 140C74 106 110 84 188 78",""],["t",194,36,"T₁",""],["t",194,59,"T₂",""],["t",194,82,"T₃",""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2022-q072","pn":72,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Surface Chemistry","st":"Hardy-Schulze rule","q":"Given below are two statements\nStatement I: In the coagulation of a negative sol, the flocculating power of the three given ions is in the order Al³⁺ > Ba²⁺ > Na⁺\nStatement II: In the coagulation of a positive sol, the flocculating power of the three given salts is in the order NaCl > Na₂SO₄ > Na₃PO₄\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct."],"c":1,"s":"By the Hardy-Schulze rule, flocculating power grows with the charge of the ion that has the opposite charge to the sol.\nFor a negative sol the cations act: Al³⁺ > Ba²⁺ > Na⁺, so Statement I is correct.\nFor a positive sol the anions act: NaCl < Na₂SO₄ < Na₃PO₄.\nStatement II gives the reverse order, so it is wrong."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q054","pn":54,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Surface Chemistry","st":"Tyndall effect","q":"The right option for the statement \"Tyndall effect is exhibited by\", is :","o":["Urea solution","NaCl solution","Glucose solution","Starch solution"],"c":3,"s":"Tyndall effect (scattering of light by particles) happens only in colloids.\nUrea, NaCl and glucose solutions are true solutions with tiny particles.\nStarch solution is a colloid, so it shows the effect."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q155","pn":155,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Surface Chemistry","st":"Colloids","q":"Measuring Zeta potential is useful in determining which property of colloidal solution ?","o":["Solubility","Stability of the colloidal particles","Size of the colloidal particles","Viscosity"],"c":1,"s":"Zeta potential is the potential difference across the fixed and diffuse layers of the charge on a colloid particle.\nA larger zeta potential means more repulsion between particles, so the sol is more stable."}
